eq 0\) in [•] then the transform is incorrect as factors corresponding to the roots \(z_j(0)\) will be missed. We are interested in the transformation properties of \(\eta_{\alpha, eta}\) because of its relation to the generalised Gibbs ensembles (GGEs) of free fermions. In [ref] the modular properties of these GGEs for free fermions was studied. These GGEs were denoted in [ref] by \[\mathop{\mathrm{Tr}}_{	ext{NS/R},\pm} \left( e^{2\pi i(	au I_1 + \sum_{n=2} \alpha_{2n-1} 	au^{2n-1} I_{2n-1})} ight)\;,\] where the conserved charges in the GGE are the KdV charges, \(I_{2n-1}\) . A definition for the traces \(\mathop{\mathrm{Tr}}_{	ext{NS/R},\pm}\) can be found in appendix A of [ref]. These GGEs are related to the generalised eta function as follows \[ egin{split} &\eta_{0,0}(	au,1,\alpha_3,\dots,\alpha_{2N-1}) = \mathop{\mathrm{Tr}}_{	ext{R},-}\left( e^{2\pi i(	au I_1 + \sum_{n=2}^N 	au^{2n-1}\alpha_{2n-1} I_{2n-1})} ight)\;,\ &\eta_{0,1/2}(	au,1,\alpha_3,\dots,\alpha_{2N-1}) = \mathop{\mathrm{Tr}}_{	ext{R},+}\left( e^{2\pi i(	au I_1 + \sum_{n=2}^N 	au^{2n-1}\alpha_{2n-1} I_{2n-1})} ight)\;,\ &\eta_{1/2,0}(	au,1,\alpha_3,\dots,\alpha_{2N-1}) = \mathop{\mathrm{Tr}}_{	ext{NS},-}\left( e^{2\pi i(	au I_1 + \sum_{n=2}^N 	au^{2n-1}\alpha_{2n-1} I_{2n-1})} ight)\;,\ &\eta_{1/2,1/2}(	au,1,\alpha_3,\dots,\alpha_{2N-1}) = \mathop{\mathrm{Tr}}_{	ext{NS},+}\left( e^{2\pi i(	au I_1 + \sum_{n=2}^N 	au^{2n-1}\alpha_{2n-1} I_{2n-1})} ight)\;. \end{split}\] In [ref] we looked at the case with just the \(I_3\) charge inserted (only \(\alpha_1\) and \(\alpha_3\) non zero) and using the thermodynamic Bethe ansatz (TBA) we conjectured the modular transformation for [•] under \(	auightarrow -1/	au\) . Our conjecture was the transformation [•] for \(\eta_{\alpha, eta}(	au,1,\alpha_3)\) . Note that in [ref] we only studied the three cases \((\alpha, eta) = \left(0, rac{1}{2}ight), \left( rac{1}{2},0ight), \left( rac{1}{2}, rac{1}{2}ight)\) but the conjecture can naturally be extended to include \((\alpha, eta)=(0,0)\) . In the companion paper [ref] we show how the transformations [•] can again be derived from the TBA. In [ref] we also provide a physical interpretation for the transformed GGEs in terms of a line defect. In this paper we give a mathematical proof for the transformation [•]. We will use the techniques from [ref] in our proof. By writing these expressions as sums over 1 dimensional lattices we can use Poisson summation to find the modular transform. This approach involves computing a Fourier transform and then deforming the integration contour to pick up poles, much in the same way one deforms the integration contour in the TBA equations to pick up the excited states. This suggests that it may be possible to adapt this method to study the GGEs of other 2d CFTs, such as the minimal models. However the eigenvalues of the KdV charges are not known in general for other theories so explicit calculations cannot be done at this stage. The paper is organised as follows. The main details of the proof of the transformation [•] are presented in section [•]. We then briefly discuss how this proof can be applied to the case with an infinite collection of non zero \(\alpha_{2n-1}\) in the GGE [•] in section [•]. Two appendices with technical results needed for the proof in section [•] have also been included.

